    "survey": "Eureka Valley Historic Context Statement (2017)",
    "source": "eureka-valley-context-statement",
    "note": "Named as the building that housed the Nasser Brothers' motion picture theatre, opened 1910, before the Castro Theater was built in 1922. The statement gives the address as 471 Castro Street in its property-type chapter and as 479 Castro Street in a footnote and in a quotation from the citywide LGBTQ context statement, which describes newly arrived residents of the 1970s buying paint, hammers and tools at Cliff's Variety here to restore run-down Victorians."
